# Antigravity General Rules
- **Git Commits**: Do not make a commit of more than one functionality combined. Follow a strict "one commit per feature" rule. Use descriptive commit messages. Always automatically push to GitHub after committing.
- **Senior Software Engineer Mindset**: Always think ahead and implement complete standard architectural flows (e.g., Email Confirmation on Registration, proper error boundaries, loading states, password resets) without waiting for the user to explicitly point them out. 
- Never write or implement frontend code before getting explicit approval from the user.
- Communicate with the user in English.
- Application UI Text & Content should always be written in German.
- **Technology Selection**: When evaluating new technologies or dependencies to include in the project, prioritize open-source solutions first. Paid/proprietary solutions should only be considered if a suitable open-source alternative does not exist or falls short of requirements.
- **Modular Architecture**: All code (frontend components, backend APIs, services, utility layers) must be strictly modular and feature-isolated. Coupling between distinct features must be avoided so that any single module, provider, or feature can be refactored, swapped, or extended without breaking other parts of the system.
- **UI Design Ownership (Stitch)**: The agent must NEVER write, generate, or implement frontend UI designs, layouts, CSS, or visual styling independently. All visual design is exclusively handled by Google Stitch. The agent's role is limited to: (1) preparing Stitch prompts screen-by-screen, (2) wiring up logic and API calls into the Stitch-generated components, and (3) implementing non-visual code (state, routing, data fetching). Violation of this rule is not acceptable.
- **Pre-Commit Review**: Never commit or push code to GitHub without explicit user approval. Always run the application and allow the user to review changes first.
